WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.329
Welcome to Class 7: Nextflow on RCC.

2
00:00:03.329 --> 00:00:07.609
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:14.266
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14.266 --> 00:00:16.644
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:16.644 --> 00:00:24.340
After this class, you can: explain what runs on the interactive node (shellhost) and what runs

6
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:30.113
on compute nodes; start a persistent Nextflow controller in tmux after release;

7
00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:35.885
use the RCC launcher and a shared project root; request CPU, memory,

8
00:00:35.885 --> 00:00:43.582
time, short-queue, scratch, array, and GPU resources; use Apptainer without placing Conda environments on shared storage;

9
00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:50.316
resume an interrupted pipeline safely; and inspect Slurm and Nextflow evidence when something fails.

10
00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:54.688
The Nextflow program remains on the interactive node (shellhost).

11
00:00:54.688 --> 00:01:01.974
It reads the pipeline graph, decides which tasks are ready, and submits them to Slurm.

12
00:01:01.974 --> 00:01:05.860
Slurm allocates CPUs, memory, time, partitions, and GPUs.

13
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.260
Scientific computation occurs only in Slurm jobs.

14
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:17.033
Compute workers execute generated task wrappers and do not need their own Java or Nextflow installation.

15
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:22.772
After the service is released, choose the project directory that owns the workflow and results:

16
00:01:22.772 --> 00:01:26.598
Replace MY_PROJECT with a directory you are authorized to use.

17
00:01:26.598 --> 00:01:32.719
The launcher creates these private per-user directories: The work directory is part of Nextflow's cache and

18
00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:33.484
resume mechanism.

19
00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:35.397
Never set it below /local.

20
00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:41.519
Local scratch is disposable and belongs to one compute job; a later task may run on

21
00:01:41.519 --> 00:01:42.284
another node.

22
00:01:42.284 --> 00:01:49.471
After release, connect to an RCC interactive node (a shellhost) and create a persistent terminal session.

23
00:01:49.471 --> 00:01:56.209
Do not start this controller on the preceding SSH gateway or inside a compute-worker allocation:

24
00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:03.397
Inside the tmux session: Detach without stopping Nextflow: Return later: A dropped laptop or jump-host connection

25
00:02:03.397 --> 00:02:06.541
then does not terminate the workflow controller.

26
00:02:06.541 --> 00:02:09.329
The copyable example is in examples/nextflow-rcc.

27
00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:14.907
After release, copy that directory into your project, enter it, and run:

28
00:02:14.907 --> 00:02:18.160
The example submits two small cpu_short jobs.

29
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:25.596
One uses the shared task work directory; one uses node-local scratch and returns only its declared

30
00:02:25.596 --> 00:02:26.061
output.

31
00:02:26.061 --> 00:02:30.243
Every example has strict CPU, memory, and time limits.

32
00:02:30.243 --> 00:02:35.820
Run it again with: The second run should reuse completed task results.

33
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:42.792
Keep the same project root, pipeline revision, inputs, and work directory for resume to succeed.

34
00:02:42.792 --> 00:02:49.099
Nextflow process directives map to Slurm requests: Request what the program can actually use.

35
00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:54.055
More CPUs or memory can increase queue time without reducing runtime.

36
00:02:54.055 --> 00:03:01.264
### Short jobs Apply the RCC label only when every task instance is guaranteed to finish

37
00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:07.571
within two hours: This selects cpu_short and a maximum task time of two hours.

38
00:03:07.571 --> 00:03:12.977
### Local scratch Use local scratch for repeated random I/O, temporary databases,

39
00:03:12.977 --> 00:03:20.186
sorting, assembly, decompressed intermediates, or very large numbers of temporary files: Nextflow creates a unique task

40
00:03:20.186 --> 00:03:27.395
directory below the Slurm job's $TMPDIR, stages inputs, executes locally, copies declared outputs back with rsync,

41
00:03:27.395 --> 00:03:30.098
and removes the task scratch directory.

42
00:03:30.098 --> 00:03:37.916
Request any standard GPU: Request the current exact RTX A6000 type: Request an architecture family:

43
00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:45.734
Request the special ARM64/GB10 platform only when the workflow supports that architecture and platform policy:

44
00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:49.382
RCC adds Apptainer --nv for these labels.

45
00:03:49.382 --> 00:03:56.158
GPU VRAM is not the same resource as host memory requested with memory.

46
00:03:56.158 --> 00:03:58.756
RCC uses Apptainer for Nextflow tasks.

47
00:03:58.756 --> 00:04:05.684
A process can declare an immutable image: Prefer a reviewed SIF file or a digest-pinned OCI

48
00:04:05.684 --> 00:04:06.117
reference.

49
00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:08.716
Do not use mutable latest tags.

50
00:04:08.716 --> 00:04:15.644
The per-user Apptainer cache is on shared project storage so a job scheduled on another node

51
00:04:15.644 --> 00:04:17.376
can reuse the image.

52
00:04:17.376 --> 00:04:20.840
Avoid Nextflow-managed Conda as the default RCC profile.

53
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.903
Conda environments contain many small files and create poor metadata-I/O patterns on shared storage.

54
00:04:26.903 --> 00:04:31.233
Use Apptainer unless a reviewed pipeline has a specific exception.

55
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:37.997
The separate bounded nf-core example pins nf-core/demo release 1.2.0, uses public synthetic test data,

56
00:04:37.997 --> 00:04:42.345
caps scheduler concurrency, and supplies teaching resources through rcc-test.config.

57
00:04:42.345 --> 00:04:48.142
Its run-demo.sh wrapper fails closed unless rcc-nextflow, apptainer, and sbatch are present.

58
00:04:48.142 --> 00:04:53.940
After release, copy both files into an approved project directory and run:

59
00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:01.670
The first run may retrieve pipeline source, public test data, and reviewed container images through the

60
00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.119
approved proxy path.

61
00:05:03.119 --> 00:05:07.950
Do not add tokens or copy credentials into the project.

62
00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:13.748
For a real pipeline, use an explicit release and its Apptainer profile:

63
00:05:13.748 --> 00:05:16.647
Replace RELEASE_TAG with a reviewed release.

64
00:05:16.647 --> 00:05:20.995
Do not implicitly follow a pipeline's changing default branch.

65
00:05:20.995 --> 00:05:21.961
Pilot a.

66
00:05:21.961 --> 00:05:29.437
After correcting the input, configuration, resource request, or software issue: Do not delete the work directory

67
00:05:29.437 --> 00:05:31.774
as a first troubleshooting step.

68
00:05:31.774 --> 00:05:37.848
Do not start several copies of the same workflow because one appears slow.

69
00:05:37.848 --> 00:05:43.455
Record: the Nextflow command and exact pipeline revision; the Nextflow run name;

70
00:05:43.455 --> 00:05:49.530
relevant .nextflow.log lines; Slurm job IDs and states; the failed task work directory;

71
00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.604
requested and observed resources; and whether the process used shared or local scratch.

72
00:05:55.604 --> 00:06:01.679
Never include passwords, tokens, private keys, or patient-identifying data in a support request.

73
00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:07.375
Before release, you have completed the preparation gate when you can explain:

74
00:06:07.375 --> 00:06:14.970
why the controller runs on an interactive node (shellhost), never an SSH gateway or worker allocation;

75
00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:22.090
why every scientific task goes through Slurm; why NXF_WORK stays on persistent shared project storage;

76
00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:28.261
when /local task scratch helps and how outputs return; why workers need Apptainer,

77
00:06:28.261 --> 00:06:33.957
but not Java or Nextflow; and which revisions, parameters, reports, image digests,

78
00:06:33.957 --> 00:06:37.280
checksums, and Slurm IDs form retained provenance.

79
00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:44.875
After RCC releases rcc-nextflow, run the bounded synthetic example and verify that -resume reuses completed work.
